=== REASONING ===
Contract for the signal_log fallback (the behavior Prompt 04 implements and
the tests below must lock down):

  R1. Live values always win. The fallback only fills VehicleState fields
      that the live store left at their Go zero value. If the live store
      already supplied a value (even an explicit zero in the future, but
      practically: any non-zero numeric or non-empty string), the fallback
      must NOT overwrite it. "Live data is fresh and authoritative; the
      signal_log read is purely a backstop." Live values are used in
      preference to signal_log every time.

  R2. Only `signal_log` is consulted. Per ADR-001, the fallback MUST call
      `database.SignalLogReader.SnapshotAt(ctx, vehicleID, time.Now())` and
      MUST NOT read from any dropped snapshot table (positions,
      state_snapshots, battery_snapshots, climate_snapshots,
      tire_pressure_snapshots, security_snapshots, location_snapshots, etc.)
      and MUST NOT introduce a query against vehicle_live_state. ADR-001 is
      respected: only signal_log, no snapshot table.

  R3. Missing signal-log entries are tolerated. If `SnapshotAt` returns a
      map that does not contain a given signal key, the corresponding
      VehicleState field stays at its Go zero value with no panic, no error,
      and no log spam. That is exactly the same observable result as
      "signal_log returned an empty map".

  R4. signal_log read failure is tolerated. If `SnapshotAt` returns a
      non-nil error, the fallback must log the error (Warn level) but must
      NOT propagate it. `BuildStateFromSignalStore` must continue to return
      a fully populated VehicleState built from whatever live values were
      available, so the calling HTTP handler can still return 200 to the
      client. The contract: read failure is tolerated, error is logged but
      not propagated, the handler can still return 200.

  R5. SignalLogReader is optional. Existing/legacy callers that construct
      `VehicleService` without wiring a SignalLogReader (i.e., `nil reader`,
      "no SignalLogReader configured") must continue to work exactly as
      today: no fallback, no panic, no nil-pointer dereference. This means
      `BuildStateFromSignalStore` must guard the reader call with a nil
      check before invoking SnapshotAt.

  R6. Field-to-signal mapping is identical to the Phase-1 live-store
      reads. The fallback must use the same Tesla signal names already used
      in vehicle_service.go Phase 1 so the merged result is consistent
      regardless of which layer supplied each field. The mapping the tests
      enumerate is:
          state.Odometer        ← snap["Odometer"]
          state.InsideTemp      ← snap["InsideTemp"]
          state.OutsideTemp     ← snap["OutsideTemp"]
          state.SoftwareVersion ← snap["Version"], else snap["SoftwareUpdateVersion"]
          state.IdealRange      ← snap["IdealBatteryRange"]
          state.RatedRange      ← snap["RatedRange"], else snap["EstBatteryRange"]
          state.Latitude        ← snap["Latitude"]
          state.Longitude       ← snap["Longitude"]
          state.BatteryLevel    ← snap["BatteryLevel"], else snap["Soc"]
          state.Speed           ← snap["VehicleSpeed"]

  R7. Test scope is BuildStateFromSignalStore only. The Phase-2
      `stateRepo.GetCurrentState` fallback for `state.State` is unchanged by
      this work and is out of scope; Prompt 04 must not regress it but the
      new tests do not need to re-cover it.

These rules together close the Phase-38 bug where pod-restart users see a
zeroed `Odometer`, `InsideTemp`, `OutsideTemp`, and empty
`SoftwareVersion` because the live store was cold. Layered live state per
ADR-007: the same merge philosophy that Prompt 02 applied across L1+L2 is
extended here across (L1∪L2)+L3-snapshot.

